  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM EDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nA shortwave trough will drop southeast from the upper midwest into \nthe Ohio valley between this afternoon through Sunday morning, \nhelping to push a cold front through the region tonight. Ahead of\nthis front, we could see some isolated strong to severe storms \ncapable of some damaging winds this afternoon through tonight. \nWhile better chances for severe storms exist to our north, we'll \nbe in the left exit region of a jet streak aloft this evening, \nwith enough shear in place to support some cluster type cell\norganization and enough instability to support some fairly strong\nstorms. Again, better chances exist to our north, but a strong to\nsevere storm can't be ruled out for any points roughly north of\nthe I-40 corridor. That being said, the chances for severe storms\nare overall on the low side. \n\nLooking ahead, the aforementioned shortwave will lead to a large \nscale upper trough deepening over the Great Lakes and eastern CONUS \nSunday into Monday before pulling away through eastern Canada and \nthe New England area on Tuesday. The subsequent northwest flow aloft \nwill keep us dry Sun through Tue before rain chances enter the \nforecast again. Dewpoints may even mix out into the 50s for both Sun \nand Mon afternoons. And while temperatures will be near seasonal \nnorms both days, the drier air would be a welcome break from the \nhumid conditions of late. As mentioned, rain chances return by \nmidweek as troughing deepens over the eastern CONUS once again. \nAdditional shortwave energy likely brings another system and \nassociated rain chances through the region Wed into Thu before \nnorthwest upper flow gives us another shot of dry and cooler air.",
